<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>Small-angle scattering is a powerful inverse structure
  determination technique, using neutrons (SANS) and X-rays (SAXS) to
  resolve nanoscale objects in their native
  environment(<xref alt="Gommes et al., 2021" rid="ref-Gommes2021beginners" ref-type="bibr">Gommes
  et al., 2021</xref>). <monospace>IQuana</monospace>
  (<inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[I(Q)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>I</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  analysis; /ɪˈɡwɑː.nə/) provides a flexible and model-agnostic Python
  framework for the fitting of analytical particle shape and interaction
  models to this reciprocal space scattering data. This reproducible and
  scriptable approach functions primarily in Jupyter notebooks, as an
  accessible interaction pathway for the
  <monospace>sasmodels</monospace>(<xref alt="Shrestha &amp; others, 2026b" rid="ref-Shrestha2026sasmodels" ref-type="bibr">Shrestha
  &amp; others, 2026b</xref>) backend library of
  <monospace>SasView</monospace>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>Over the past few decades, small-angle scattering analysis has
  evolved from pioneering early CLI software packages such as
  <monospace>FISH</monospace>, into predominantly GUI-centric efforts,
  with prominent examples including <monospace>SasView</monospace>,
  <monospace>SASfit</monospace>, <monospace>Irena</monospace>,
  <monospace>ATSAS</monospace> and <monospace>McSAS3</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Breßler et al., 2015" rid="ref-bressler2015sasfit" ref-type="bibr">Breßler
  et al., 2015</xref>;
  <xref alt="Franke et al., 2025" rid="ref-Franke2025ATSAS" ref-type="bibr">Franke
  et al., 2025</xref>;
  <xref alt="Ilavsky &amp; Jemian, 2009" rid="ref-ilavsky2009irena" ref-type="bibr">Ilavsky
  &amp; Jemian, 2009</xref>;
  <xref alt="Pauw &amp; Breßler, 2026" rid="ref-pauw2026mcsas3" ref-type="bibr">Pauw
  &amp; Breßler, 2026</xref>;
  <xref alt="Shrestha &amp; others, 2026c" rid="ref-Shrestha2026SasView" ref-type="bibr">Shrestha
  &amp; others, 2026c</xref>).</p>
  <p>Such initiatives have been powerful drivers of progress and allow
  effective exploration of datasets. On the flipside, these packages
  present their own respective drawbacks, such as challenges in
  reproducibility and repeatability, automation and ease of scripting.
  Tools are now in place for users to implement their own preferred
  analysis workflows, if they are willing to assemble data objects,
  model kernels, parameterization, resolution functions and calls to the
  optimizer– in other words, spending resource on reimplementing core
  functionality which has been accrued across the major packages from
  years of frontier expertise.</p>
  <p>We therefore pitch <monospace>IQuana</monospace> at groups
  including researchers, educators, students, and developers. We
  envisage these groups to use <monospace>IQuana</monospace> to
  construct reproducible, version-controlled analysis records to better
  meet today’s FAIR data
  demands.(<xref alt="Wilkinson et al., 2016" rid="ref-FAIRdata" ref-type="bibr">Wilkinson
  et al., 2016</xref>) Didactically, we believe there is power in this
  tool which encourages the user to understand the chain of scientific
  decisions being made in the analysis process, exposing features which
  may be treated as black boxes in fully-fledged GUI software,
  i.e. <monospace>load_data</monospace> &gt;
  <monospace>set_model</monospace> &gt; <monospace>set_param</monospace>
  &gt; <monospace>fit</monospace>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>Arguably the most widely-used open source software package for
  small-angle scattering data analysis and model fitting is
  <monospace>SasView</monospace> and its accompanying model library
  <monospace>sasmodels</monospace>, which provides a large library of
  form and structure factors via <monospace>sasdata</monospace>. The
  <monospace>SasView</monospace> ecosystem therefore provides native
  support for common file formats, polydispersity, and instrument
  resolution, and a (verbose) API. Comparable toolboxes are provided by
  <monospace>ATSAS</monospace>,(<xref alt="Franke et al., 2025" rid="ref-Franke2025ATSAS" ref-type="bibr">Franke
  et al., 2025</xref>) <monospace>SASfit</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Breßler et al., 2015" rid="ref-bressler2015sasfit" ref-type="bibr">Breßler
  et al., 2015</xref>),
  <monospace>Irena</monospace>(<xref alt="Ilavsky &amp; Jemian, 2009" rid="ref-ilavsky2009irena" ref-type="bibr">Ilavsky
  &amp; Jemian, 2009</xref>), <monospace>McSAS3</monospace>(@
  <xref alt="Pauw &amp; Breßler, 2026" rid="ref-pauw2026mcsas3" ref-type="bibr">Pauw
  &amp; Breßler, 2026</xref>), and to some extent the NIST macros for
  Igor Pro remain in
  use(<xref alt="Kline, 2006" rid="ref-kline2006" ref-type="bibr">Kline,
  2006</xref>). It is possible to leverage general-purpose libraries
  such as <monospace>SciPy</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Virtanen et al., 2020" rid="ref-virtanen2020scipy" ref-type="bibr">Virtanen
  et al., 2020</xref>) and <monospace>lmfit</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Newville et al., 2014" rid="ref-lmfit" ref-type="bibr">Newville
  et al., 2014</xref>) for these tasks, but key aspects of the
  underlying scattering physics and file handling must be implemented by
  the user.</p>
  <p>Fundamentally, <monospace>IQuana</monospace> adopts a philosophy of
  not reinventing and reimplementing scattering models, optimizers, file
  handling and visualization. Instead, we build directly on
  <monospace>SasView</monospace> componentry for a notebook-first
  implementation which uses <monospace>sasmodels</monospace> for
  analytical fitting functions,
  <monospace>sasdata</monospace>(<xref alt="Shrestha &amp; others, 2026a" rid="ref-Shrestha2026sasdata" ref-type="bibr">Shrestha
  &amp; others, 2026a</xref>) for handling of small-angle scattering
  data files in community standard formats such as (NX)canSAS, and
  <monospace>BUMPS</monospace>, which provides the underlying
  reproducible optimizer
  framework.(<xref alt="Kienzle &amp; others, 2026" rid="ref-bumps" ref-type="bibr">Kienzle
  &amp; others, 2026</xref>)</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>We take the primary design consideration that the state of the
  analysis should be explicit and open to user evaluation: one must be
  able to inspect plots without altering fit results, and to be warned
  of potential fitting pitfalls (e.g. a fitted parameter hitting a
  bound, or data missing resolution information), without software
  silence. Results are provided as rich display objects generally using
  Plotly
  (<xref alt="Plotly Technologies Inc., 2015" rid="ref-plotly" ref-type="bibr">Plotly
  Technologies Inc., 2015</xref>) and not limited by a fixed GUI
  implementation, but also given as simple plain Python data, allowing
  easy onward machine readability.</p>
  <p>The <monospace>IQuana</monospace> class acts as a facade for
  underlying functionality. Data (such as CSV, canSAS XML, NXcanSAS HDF5
  including multiple-dataset files) are loaded via
  <monospace>sasdata</monospace>, which brings basic resolution function
  handling and weighting of residuals; <monospace>IQuana</monospace> has
  an independent implementation of common dataset arithmetic operations
  (subtraction, multiplication, etc). A parameter manager then handles
  the fitting parameters in a manner consistent wth the loaded
  <monospace>sasmodels</monospace> kernel. Fitting engine access is
  provided using the same framework for <monospace>BUMPS</monospace> and
  <monospace>SciPy</monospace>, which therefore return directly
  comparable results. A <monospace>FitReport</monospace> is generated,
  which can be rendered as a notebook table, alongside a
  <monospace>Plotly</monospace> figure showing data, fits, and
  residuals.</p>
  <p>There are a number of useful features conferred by the
  <monospace>IQuana</monospace> design approach, many of which are
  increasingly pertinent to modern small-angle scattering analysis
  problems. For example, after loading their data and subtracting a
  background, a user might take advantage of the
  <monospace>pr_inversion</monospace> functionality to obtain Indirect
  Fourier Transforms (IFT) of their data, and then model their
  experimental <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[I(Q)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>I</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  with combined <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[P(Q) * S(Q)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>P</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>*</mml:mo><mml:mi>S</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  or apply multiple <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[P(Q)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>P</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  concomitantly. One of the most important features offered by
  <monospace>IQuana</monospace> is the opportunity to trivially perform
  simultaneous constrained fits, for example across a contrast series
  using <monospace>MultiFitter</monospace>; we expose simple
  functionality for parameters to be independent, fixed, linked, or
  shared between several datasets in a series. Typical polydispersity
  functions (Gaussian lognormal, Schulz, rectangular or Boltzmann) can
  be introduced via <monospace>set_pd_param()</monospace> and fitted
  alongside other parameters. <monospace>set_q_range()</monospace>
  allows a user to restrict their fitting window, for example to avoid
  biasing a fit result for data containing high-Q Bragg peaks and a
  simple form factor at low-Q. The residual weighting can be adjusted
  using <monospace>set_weighting</monospace> to follow
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{d}I]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">d</mml:mi><mml:mi>I</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  (default), <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\sqrt{|I|}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msqrt><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:mi>I</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msqrt></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[|I|]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:mi>I</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  or unit weights. Moreover, <monospace>IQuana</monospace> supports
  custom plugin models and not just the built-in library. All of the
  above can be approached with further statistical rigour via
  <monospace>fit_bayesian()</monospace>, which implements the
  <monospace>DREAM</monospace>
  sampler(<xref alt="Vrugt et al., 2009" rid="ref-Vrugt2009DREAM" ref-type="bibr">Vrugt
  et al., 2009</xref>) for a MCMC approach. We envisage that a user
  could either directly upload their fitting notebook or an exported
  JSON from <monospace>save_analysis()</monospace> as part of a
  publication, facilitating FAIR reproduction and allowing a reader to
  more nimbly follow their fitting workflow. The full
  <monospace>IQuana</monospace> feature set is comprehensively described
  in the documentation, which includes a complete user guide, example
  data, as well as full walkthroughs both for basic functions and
  advanced features such as simultaneous constrained fitting and using
  custom models.</p>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>As found in the <monospace>IQuana</monospace> documented
    notebook examples, fits and residuals from using
    <monospace>IQuana</monospace> upon synthetic SANS test data; (a) a
    spherical form factor multiplied by a hard-sphere structure factor;
    (b) a fit of four convoluted form factors; (c) a posterior pair
    (corner) plot from a <monospace>DREAM</monospace> algorithm call to
    <monospace>fit_bayesian</monospace> for a spherical form
    factor.</p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="iquana-image.png" />
  </fig>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p><monospace>IQuana</monospace> began life as a pilot project,
  <monospace>sans-fitter</monospace> (released January 2026), and has
  since evolved into a much more full-fledged repository. The intended
  audience is research staff for primary scientific research with
  wide-reaching potential impact. Beyond this, we see
  <monospace>IQuana</monospace> as an pedagogical tool, which has
  already been used effectively in summer schools and undergraduate
  teaching curricula, for conveying challenging concepts around the
  analysis of small-angle scattering data.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>Human authors made all core design decisions, and reviewed, edited,
  and validated all AI-assisted outputs used in the development of this
  software. Tools used include:</p>
  <p>Anthropic Claude Fable 5.1: help with architectural design;
  Anthropic Claude Opus 5.5: help with refactoring and unit test
  coverage; Anthropic Claude Sonnet 5.5: help with documentation
  proofing; Codacy https://app.codacy.com : help with testing coverage
  metrics; CodeRabbit AI: https://www.coderabbit.ai : help with code and
  documentation review.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements">
  <title>Acknowledgements</title>
  <p>We acknowledge the developers of <monospace>SasView</monospace>,
  <monospace>sasmodels</monospace>, <monospace>sasdata</monospace> and
  <monospace>BUMPS</monospace>, on whose work
  <monospace>IQuana</monospace> is built.</p>
</sec>
</body>
